/**
 * Closed-won revenue rollups for the current calendar year.
 *
 * All totals are produced by a single aggregate SOQL query (SUM ... GROUP BY);
 * individual Opportunity records are never loaded. The class is declared
 * "with sharing", so the aggregate only counts opportunities the running user
 * is allowed to see.
 */
public with sharing class OpportunityRollupService {

    /**
     * Returns the running calendar year's closed-won revenue per account.
     *
     * The value for an account is SUM(Amount) over its opportunities where
     * IsWon = true and CloseDate falls in the current calendar year. Every
     * requested (non-null) account Id is always a key of the result, mapped to
     * 0 when it has no qualifying opportunities or when every qualifying
     * Amount is blank/null. Ids that were not requested are never keys.
     *
     * @param accountIds the Account Ids to summarize; may be null or empty
     * @return a map keyed by every requested non-null Account Id; never null,
     *         and never containing a null value
     */
    public static Map<Id, Decimal> wonAmountThisYear(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        Map<Id, Decimal> revenueByAccountId = new Map<Id, Decimal>();

        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return revenueByAccountId;
        }

        // Seed every requested Account with 0 so no value can come back null,
        // and build the clean bind set used by the aggregate query.
        Set<Id> idsToQuery = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id accountId : accountIds) {
            if (accountId != null) {
                revenueByAccountId.put(accountId, 0);
                idsToQuery.add(accountId);
            }
        }

        if (idsToQuery.isEmpty()) {
            return revenueByAccountId;
        }

        // Single aggregate query: no Opportunity rows are loaded into the heap.
        for (AggregateResult ar : [
            SELECT AccountId, SUM(Amount) totalAmount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE IsWon = true
              AND CloseDate = THIS_YEAR
              AND AccountId IN :idsToQuery
            GROUP BY AccountId
        ]) {
            Id accountId = (Id) ar.get('AccountId');
            Decimal total = (Decimal) ar.get('totalAmount');
            // SUM over an all-blank group comes back null; normalise to 0.
            if (total == null) {
                total = 0;
            }
            revenueByAccountId.put(accountId, total);
        }

        return revenueByAccountId;
    }
}
```

## Approach

**Step 1 — create three Contacts, all-or-nothing.**
The sObject Collections *create* endpoint (`POST /composite/sObjects`) accepts an array of mixed‑type records and an `allOrNone` flag. With `"allOrNone": true`, Salesforce validates and saves all three Contacts in a single transaction: any error rolls the whole request back, so you either get three `201 Created` results or nothing.

**Step 2 — two independent updates on different object types.**
The collections *update* endpoint (`PATCH /composite/sObjects`) takes its sObject type from the URL, so it can't update an Account and an Opportunity in the same call. The right tool is the **Composite resource** (`POST /composite`), which runs a list of subrequests sequentially and **independently** — each subrequest is executed and reported on its own, so a failure on the Account (e.g. validation rule, field‑level security) does not prevent the Opportunity `NextStep` update from being saved. Subrequests use `PATCH /sobjects/{type}/{id}` with `referenceId`s so the response can be correlated per subrequest.

