public class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    // Any character that is not a digit or a plus sign is stripped out.
    // The pattern is compiled once per transaction instead of once per record.
    private static final Pattern NON_DIALER_CHARACTERS = Pattern.compile('[^0-9+]');

    /**
     * Normalizes Phone on the supplied contacts so it contains only digits and a
     * leading plus sign, then saves every record in a single partial-success DML.
     *
     * @param contactIds Contacts to process (duplicates/nulls tolerated, up to 200).
     * @return Map of contact Id -> first error message for records that could not be
     *         saved. Empty when every record saved successfully.
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> failuresById = new Map<Id, String>();

        // Guard clause: nothing to do, and it keeps us out of the query/DML limits.
        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failuresById;
        }

        // De-duplicate and drop nulls so the SOQL bind and the DML stay clean.
        Set<Id> idSet = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id contactId : contactIds) {
            if (contactId != null) {
                idSet.add(contactId);
            }
        }
        if (idSet.isEmpty()) {
            return failuresById;
        }

        // Only Id and Phone are queried, and only rows that actually have a phone are
        // touched - contacts without a phone are left out entirely (not updated, and
        // therefore never able to appear as a failure).
        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact con : [SELECT Id, Phone FROM Contact WHERE Id IN :idSet]) {
            if (String.isBlank(con.Phone)) {
                continue;
            }
            con.Phone = NON_DIALER_CHARACTERS.matcher(con.Phone).replaceAll('');
            contactsToUpdate.add(con);
        }

        if (contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return failuresById;
        }

        // Single bulk DML statement for the whole batch; allOrNone = false so one
        // validation-rule failure does not roll back the other records.
        for (Database.SaveResult sr : Database.update(contactsToUpdate, false)) {
            if (sr.isSuccess()) {
                continue;
            }
            String message = 'Unknown error';
            if (!sr.getErrors().isEmpty()) {
                message = sr.getErrors()[0].getMessage();
            }
            failuresById.put(sr.getId(), message);
        }

        return failuresById;
    }
}
```

### Notes — things I adjusted or added, and why

1. **Guard clauses for `null`/empty input and for an all-null Id list.** The signature allows `null`; without the guard you would bind `null` into SOQL and issue an unnecessary query. Cost: two early `return`s of an empty map.
2. **De-duplicate Ids into a `Set<Id>` before querying.** Caller-supplied lists can contain duplicates; without this you could try to save the same row twice in one statement (a "duplicate id in update list" error) and inflate row counts.
3. **Only queried `Id` and `Phone`.** Selecting `SELECT ... FROM Contact` (all fields) or a `Map<Id, Contact>` would pull far more data than needed for this job; it also risks tripping field-size/heap limits at 200 records.
4. **Used `String.isBlank()` rather than `== null`.** A whitespace-only `Phone` is treated as "no phone" and left out, which matches the intent of the dialer cleanup.
5. **Compiled the regex once as a `private static final Pattern`.** `String.replaceAll()` recompiles the pattern per call; using a precompiled `Pattern` with `matcher().replaceAll('')` does the same job with less overhead inside the loop (200 iterations).
6. **Skipped issuing the DML when nothing matched.** `Database.update(new List<Contact>(), false)` would burn a DML statement and a row count for no benefit.
7. **Read `sr.getErrors()[0]` only after checking `!sr.isSuccess()`, with an empty-errors fallback.** This is what actually produces the "first error message" you asked for, and it avoids an unhandled index error if Salesforce ever returns a result with no error array.
8. **Deliberately left the class in system mode (no sharing keyword) and did not wrap the DML in `Security.stripInaccessible`.** This is a maintenance routine operating on an explicitly supplied list of Ids; with `with sharing` or CRUD/FLS stripping, an inaccessible contact would be silently dropped from the query — it would be neither updated nor reported in your failure map, which is worse than updating it. If you'd rather it honour record access, say so and I'll switch it to `with sharing` and document that dropped rows are expected.
9. **Did *not* skip records whose phone is already normalized.** You said "on each contact that has a phone" → "save them all", so every non-blank phone is included. If you want to minimize DML rows, an `if (con.Phone == normalized) continue;` line would cut the update set substantially; failure reporting is unaffected either way.

Governors: 1 SOQL query, 1 DML statement, ≤200 DML rows, well within limits for a 200-record batch.

