public class FollowUpTaskService {

    /**
     * Creates a follow-up Task for each of the given Opportunities.
     *
     * Each Task is inserted on its own inside a try/catch so that a validation-rule
     * rejection on one Task does not prevent the others from being created.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds the Opportunities from the weekly pipeline review
     * @return the Ids of the Opportunities whose Task could not be created
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOppIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOppIds;
        }

        // De-duplicate so we never create two identical follow-ups for one Opportunity.
        Set<Id> requestedIds = new Set<Id>(opportunityIds);
        requestedIds.remove(null);

        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunitiesById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>([
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
              FROM Opportunity
             WHERE Id IN :requestedIds
        ]);

        // Anything we were handed that is not a readable Opportunity cannot get a Task,
        // so report it rather than silently claiming success.
        failedOppIds.addAll(requestedIds);
        failedOppIds.removeAll(opportunitiesById.keySet());

        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(7);

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunitiesById.values()) {
            Task followUp = new Task(
                WhatId      = opp.Id,
                OwnerId     = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject     = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status      = 'Not Started',
                Priority    = 'Normal'
            );

            try {
                insert followUp;
            } catch (DmlException e) {
                // Validation rules (and anything else) land here; keep going.
                failedOppIds.add(opp.Id);
            }
        }

        return failedOppIds;
    }
}
```

---

### What I would do differently, and why

**1. The specified version blows the DML-statement governor limit before it finishes.**
One `insert` statement per Opportunity means 150–250 separate DML statements in a single call. The limit is **150 DML statements per transaction**, so as soon as the review covers 151 opportunities the method throws `System.LimitException: Too many DML statements: 250` — and because that is an uncatchable limit exception, it kills the whole transaction. None of the tasks are created, and the caller gets an error instead of a "these five failed" set. This is the main reason I would not ship the loop-of-inserts version for this workload.

**2. A single bulk insert with `allOrNone = false` gives exactly the same behaviour, without the limit problem.**
`Database.insert(tasks, false)` records per-row results: rejected rows come back in `saveResult` with `success == false` and an `errors` list, accepted rows are committed, and nothing throws. It reproduces the requested semantics (one bad Task never blocks the others) with **one** DML statement instead of N, plus one SOQL query.

```apex
public class FollowUpTaskService {

    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOppIds = new Set<Id>();
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOppIds;
        }

        Set<Id> requestedIds = new Set<Id>(opportunityIds);
        requestedIds.remove(null);

        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunitiesById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>([
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId FROM Opportunity WHERE Id IN :requestedIds
        ]);

        failedOppIds.addAll(requestedIds);
        failedOppIds.removeAll(opportunitiesById.keySet());

        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(7);
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        List<Id> rowOppIds = new List<Id>(); // positional map row -> opportunity

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunitiesById.values()) {
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId       = opp.Id,
                OwnerId      = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject      = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status       = 'Not Started',
                Priority     = 'Normal'
            ));
            rowOppIds.add(opp.Id);
        }

        if (tasks.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOppIds;
        }

        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasks, false);
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedOppIds.add(rowOppIds[i]);
            }
        }
        return failedOppIds;
    }
}
```

