public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {
    /**
     * Returns a list the same size and order as `leads`: for each lead, the Id of the
     * first contact (in `contacts` order) whose email equals the lead's email, or null.
     *
     * Matching rules:
     *  - case-insensitive and whitespace-insensitive (leading/trailing spaces trimmed)
     *  - blank/whitespace-only emails never match
     *  - first match in `contacts` order wins
     *
     * Pure in-memory method: no SOQL, no DML. Safe for use inside the import transaction.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> matches = new List<Id>();

        if (leads == null || leads.isEmpty()) {
            return matches;
        }

        if (contacts == null || contacts.isEmpty()) {
            // Every lead simply has no match.
            for (Lead lead : leads) {
                matches.add(null);
            }
            return matches;
        }

        for (Lead lead : leads) {
            Id matchId = null;
            String leadEmail = normalizeEmail(lead == null ? null : lead.Email);

            if (String.isBlank(leadEmail)) {
                matches.add(null);
                continue;
            }

            for (Contact contact : contacts) {
                if (contact == null) {
                    continue;
                }
                String contactEmail = normalizeEmail(contact.Email);
                if (String.isBlank(contactEmail)) {
                    continue;
                }
                if (contactEmail.equals(leadEmail)) {
                    matchId = contact.Id;
                    break; // first match in `contacts` order wins
                }
            }

            matches.add(matchId);
        }

        return matches;
    }

    private static String normalizeEmail(String email) {
        if (email == null) {
            return null;
        }
        String trimmed = email.trim();
        return trimmed.isEmpty() ? null : trimmed.toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

On "would I do it differently": for these sizes (≤3,000 × ≤3,000 = up to 9M comparisons worst case) I'd still prefer a normalized-email → first-contact-Id map built once from `contacts`, giving O(n + m) and no dependence on list order being re-scanned per lead — but that trades a little readability and requires deterministic "first in `contacts` order" seeding (only put the Id if the key isn't already present). Given the stated volumes and that the import is a single short-lived transaction, the nested loop is fine, readable, and avoids building an intermediate map; I'd only switch to the map approach if either list could grow much larger or the method were called repeatedly in a loop. The null/blank/trim/case handling above is the part I'd insist on regardless of approach, since that's where correctness bugs actually come from.

